{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1137877-new-orleans-cause-i-need-ideas","id":1137877,"title":"New Orleans 'cause I need ideas.","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mage: The Awakening"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-09-05T17:31:56-07:00","id":1137877,"title":"New Orleans 'cause I need ideas.","content":"So, I'm in a Mage game that is taking place in New Orleans. What I'd like to hear is ideas on a central Mystery of New Orleans that Mages would gravitate to. In particular I'm interested in ideas that relate to Fate because that's an idea I've hit upon. I have this feeling that New Orleans is a place that has a tendency to generate Destiny. Big things have come out of New Orleans because it was Destiny, or some functions of Destiny are cyclical (Explaining why Mardis Gras happens there every year), or Destiny in New Orleans -has- to be fulfilled even if you're dead (Explaining the huge number of ghosts). It's kind of like the Rune Walks in London, except instead of weird magical effects that might empower your spells it has a specifically Fate-ish effect instead. So, how does one gain a Destiny by being in New Orleans? How has this confluence of Destiny application to to New Orleans affected its development? How can that be manipulated or how is it understood by the Mages who live there? What are the effects of confluence of Destiny in the city? What are the benefits? What are the negatives? What do you think Mages understand about it at this point? Etc. Your input is welcome and thanks in advance.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2632,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","user":"Mrmdubois","date":". 09-05-2017, 08:37 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-05T17:58:05-07:00","id":1137884,"title":"","content":"Why there arent vampires in the city no more? *ba dum dish*<br /> <br /> Mages believe there is an underground market of one or more melancholy carnival....iers in the city trading destinies or good luck charms for a gruesome price. The truth is more complex, a summoning of fate (i dont remember then name of the fate summonings) a huge one twist destinies to those that a follow a seeminly random path in the city (pattern becomes more clear when one look at the colonial map of the city. <br /> <br /> A balance must be kept so as a good destiny is granted another horrible fate is handed. But is it though? Master melancholiers would agree that the balance doesnt add up, better destinies are granted than their worse counterparts. There is a built up going up and the bill is overdue.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","name":"LokiRavenSpeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-05T23:11:34-07:00","id":1137928,"title":"","content":"Far too many years ago I spent several months living in New Orleans and even longer in southern Louisiana and to me the place always felt like theater. From the landscapes to the architecture, history and people it all felt like it was all supposed to be part of some grand production.<br /> \u200bHow about the mystery of New Orleans is a play or story that is repeatedly played out in the region, repeating themes if not absolute reproductions, and the Destinies being handed out are actually for people chosen to play some part, the hero and the fool, villain and wise man, the innocent and the beast.<br /> \u200bThe Mages of the city have realized this and learned that with effort they can not only make accurate predictions about where the story will take the city next, but even influence the details as long as they don't attempt to derail the play itself.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6084-2pttakrill","name":"2ptTakrill","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"942"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-06T10:46:26-07:00","id":1138049,"title":"","content":"It is the work of the Chainsmith. Some people think it is an angel. Some believe it is a demon from the Pit. Some even think it is a different thing altogether: a wild-eyed Warlock theorize about the Great Sympathetic Attractor and how it entangles both destinies and sympathetic links of all the people that comes to New Orleans, or already live there. A voudoun cult claims it is a favor from Papa Legba, Opener of the Ways, or a prank from trickster Kalfu. Some God-Machine follower believe everything is a master plan of Infrastructure, soon to be completed. <br /> <br /> The Chainsmith is a myth, many says. Our destinies are still our own, just like everywhere else, they say. Yet the stories keep coming: the American Dream come true for a farmer that found a winning ticket, only to end up living in the streets without a cent 3 months later. A mediocre guitar player steals his talent from an accomplished artist, marries the man's daughter and buys the man's house after foreclosure, but he end up dead from a bullet intended for the fallen artist. 'The End is Nigh!', raves a street preacher; he lost his parish to a shadow man with a rotten smile, when people began to ignore him and went to the stranger instead for spiritual counsel, until he became a cult leader that bought the desecrated church and turned it into a Coaching program center.<br /> <br /> There is more, if you care enough to listen to the whispers in the night. These stories speak of a body-hopping monster, or a body-hopping curse. A Lick wakes up confused during the day, he is in another creature's body, a patchwork man with a fire in his belly. He watches in horror as the creature that stole his body tries to reach him but burns in the sun; his last words were drowned by the clubs and jackboots of a biker gang that accused him of burning the poor guy a live and proceeded to kill him in bloody payback. A Nameless mage swears he lived a whole week as a demon, running from the heavenly host, wearing and discarding identities like one changes clothes. He changed back and found himself back in his own body, hidden in a bolthole in the ground and half-mad from starvation. An old Bound confessed having killed his former krewemates after having made a pact with a wolf-demon and hunting them down alongside a pack of demon-wolves; the taste of flesh of his former friends never left his mind, it was too good to be true.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/216-zooroos","name":"Zooroos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar216_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"250"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}